   Manny came in twelve minutes later in slippers and a Knicks T-shirt
with a bag of ice in one hand and an expression of enormous, be‐
wildered loyalty. “You said ‘I did a thing,’” he said. “You didn’t say
what thing.”
   “I broke his ear.”
   “Okay.” He crouched, slowly, ribs complaining, and handed Danny
the ice with an air of great ceremony. “Hi. I’m Manny. This is the
dumbest person I know. He’s going to bring you a Gatorade.”
   I brought a Gatorade from the bodega, which was open, because the
bodega is always open. When I came back, Danny had the ice against
his ear and Manny was telling him about the time he’d accidentally
played a wedding in the wrong key for two hours and nobody noticed.
   “They noticed,” said Danny.
   “They noticed. They were polite.”
   I braced against the radiator under the window.
   The room slid.
   The same room, 1951. A long table in place of the folding one, and
on it a bank of brass jacks and cords, and eleven women in headsets sit‐
ting in a row like a choir. A wall clock. A banner: THE BRONX EX‐
CHANGE. The room smelled of cigarettes and hot dust and coffee.
Cables rose and fell in the air like reeds.
   The operator at the end of the row was perhaps thirty, with her hair
pinned up and a ring on a chain around her neck. She was holding the
line to somebody in trouble. I could hear nothing of the caller, only her
side.



